---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import StudyHero from "../../components/study/StudyHero.astro";
import StudyGlance from "../../components/study/StudyGlance.astro";
import StudyHead from "../../components/study/StudyHead.astro";
import StudyMedia from "../../components/study/StudyMedia.astro";
import StudyQuote from "../../components/study/StudyQuote.astro";
import StudyCta from "../../components/study/StudyCta.astro";
import StudyNext from "../../components/study/StudyNext.astro";

const jsonLd = [
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "CreativeWork",
    "name": "VBooking — One travel platform, two sides",
    "description": "A traveler community with AI trip planning and real travel experts, and a workspace for agents that unifies booking, a CRM pipeline and the Ask Genie AI assistant.",
    "url": "https://abdulazizabudhair.com/work/vbooking",
    "image": "https://abdulazizabudhair.com/assets/img/og-vbooking.jpg",
    "author": { "@type": "Person", "name": "Abdulaziz Abu Dhair", "url": "https://abdulazizabudhair.com/" },
    "creator": { "@type": "Person", "name": "Abdulaziz Abu Dhair", "url": "https://abdulazizabudhair.com/" },
    "genre": "Travel tech · B2C + B2B",
    "inLanguage": "en"
  },
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
    "itemListElement": [
      { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Home", "item": "https://abdulazizabudhair.com/" },
      { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "Work", "item": "https://abdulazizabudhair.com/#work" },
      { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 3, "name": "VBooking", "item": "https://abdulazizabudhair.com/work/vbooking" }
    ]
  }
];
---
<Layout
  title="VBooking — Case Study · Abdulaziz Abu Dhair"
  description="A traveler community with AI trip planning and real travel experts, and a workspace for agents that unifies booking, a CRM pipeline and the Ask Genie AI assistant — designed end to end as one system."
  path="/work/vbooking"
  alternate="/ar/work/vbooking"
  ogType="article"
  ogImage="og-vbooking.jpg"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
  study
>
<article class="study" aria-labelledby="case-title">
  <StudyHero
    project="vbooking"
    eyebrow="Case study · Travel tech · B2C + B2B"
    title="VBooking"
    subtitle="One travel platform, two sides — travelers and travel agents"
    lead="A traveler community with AI trip planning and real travel experts, and a workspace for agents that unifies booking, a CRM pipeline and the Ask Genie AI assistant."
    facts={[
      { label: "My role", value: "Lead UX/UI Designer, solo end-to-end" },
      { label: "Platform", value: "Community (web + mobile), Turbo Suite for agents, and the marketing site" },
      { label: "Timeline", value: "11 months · Aug 2024 – Jun 2025" },
      { label: "Status", value: "Both sides live in beta", status: true },
    ]}
    stackFacts
    coverAlt="VBooking Community trip page for a 7-day Tokyo and Japan city adventure, over the Turbo Suite CRM pipeline."
  />

  <StudyGlance items={[
    { label: "Scope", value: "2 platforms", text: "Community for travelers + Turbo Suite for agents" },
    { label: "Status", value: "Live beta", text: "Live in beta for travelers and agents" },
  ]} />

  <section class="study-section study-overview" aria-labelledby="overview-title">
    <div class="container study-overview__grid">
      <div class="study-overview__head">
        <p class="eyebrow"><span class="eyebrow__square" aria-hidden="true"></span>The challenge</p>
        <h2 class="study-overview__title" id="overview-title">Travelers want trust.<br>Agents want speed.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="study-overview__body">
        <div>
          <h3>Context</h3>
          <p>VBooking set out to unify a fragmented travel industry under one platform with two sides: Community, where travelers plan and book trips, and Turbo Suite, where travel agents build and sell them. Both sides meet on the same trip — a request from a traveler in Community lands as a lead in an agent’s pipeline.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Problem</h3>
          <p>Travelers didn’t know whose advice to trust, and putting a full trip together meant jumping between disconnected sites. Agents had the mirror problem: separate tools for search, quoting, CRM and finance, prices that went stale, and no single place to follow a lead from first contact to payment.</p>
        </div>
        <ul class="study-sides">
          <li class="fact"><span class="fact__label">Community · For travelers</span><p>Trust, and one place to plan.</p></li>
          <li class="fact"><span class="fact__label">Turbo Suite · For agents</span><p>Speed, and control over their own numbers.</p></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="study-section study-section--muted" aria-labelledby="community-title">
    <div class="container">
      <StudyHead id="community-title" eyebrow="01 — Community · For travelers" line1="Three ways into a trip — and a real" line2="person behind every quote" lead="The gap wasn’t information — it was trust, and the lack of one recognized route to a real answer." accent={false} />
      <ol class="study-decisions">
        <li class="fact fact--md"><span class="fact__label">01</span><h3 class="fact__value">Three ways into planning</h3><p class="fact__text">AI, manual, or a real travel expert — from the same entry point, after exploring three layouts for surfacing the choice and reviewing them with stakeholders. A single buried link meant people defaulted to browsing alone.</p></li>
        <li class="fact fact--md"><span class="fact__label">02</span><h3 class="fact__value">AI draft, human price</h3><p class="fact__text">The AI planner produces a full day-by-day itinerary with a map before asking for payment, then “Send Request” turns that draft into a real, human-priced offer.</p></li>
        <li class="fact fact--md"><span class="fact__label">03</span><h3 class="fact__value">Destination pages as one hub</h3><p class="fact__text">Hotels, activities, restaurants, car rental and articles together — instead of scattered across sections.</p></li>
        <li class="fact fact--md"><span class="fact__label">04</span><h3 class="fact__value">Experts with a public profile</h3><p class="fact__text">Reviews, followers and their own packages, so “who do I trust” is answered with the social proof people already rely on.</p></li>
      </ol>
      <div class="study-pair study-pair--after">
        <figure class="study-figure">
          <StudyMedia project="vbooking" name="02-destination-hub-tokyo" desktop={[588, 477]} tablet={[704, 571]} mobile={[358, 288]} alt="VBooking destination hub for Tokyo with facts, AI summary and shortcuts to hotels and things to do." />
          <figcaption>Destination hub for Tokyo — facts, AI summary and shortcuts to hotels, things to do and more.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="study-figure">
          <StudyMedia project="vbooking" name="03-travel-expert-profile" desktop={[588, 477]} tablet={[704, 571]} mobile={[358, 288]} alt="VBooking travel expert profile with ratings, followers and packages." />
          <figcaption>Travel expert profile — ratings, followers and packages.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="study-section" aria-labelledby="turbo-title">
    <div class="container">
      <StudyHead id="turbo-title" eyebrow="02 — Turbo Suite · For travel agents" line1="One workspace from first contact" line2="to payment" lead="Agents don’t want delight — they want speed and control over their own numbers. Every decision was weighed against “does this save a step”." accent={false} />
      <ol class="study-decisions">
        <li class="fact fact--md"><span class="fact__label">01</span><h3 class="fact__value">One CRM pipeline</h3><p class="fact__text">Every incoming lead in one Kanban view, so an agent never checks two systems to know where a deal stands.</p></li>
        <li class="fact fact--md"><span class="fact__label">02</span><h3 class="fact__value">Real-time numbers up front</h3><p class="fact__text">Inventory and financial exposure — unpaid orders, contract limits, cancellations — on the home dashboard, since stale numbers were the exact complaint.</p></li>
        <li class="fact fact--md"><span class="fact__label">03</span><h3 class="fact__value">Itinerary builder, day by day</h3><p class="fact__text">Flights, hotels, restaurants, transport — with a “Generate with AI” shortcut, so a client’s ask becomes a priced package in minutes.</p></li>
        <li class="fact fact--md"><span class="fact__label">04</span><h3 class="fact__value">Ask Genie as a first-class tab</h3><p class="fact__text">Not a side panel: agents describe what a customer needs in plain language and get offers back immediately.</p></li>
      </ol>
      <figure class="study-figure study-figure--after-facts">
        <StudyMedia project="vbooking" name="05-turbo-crm-pipeline" desktop={[1200, 775]} tablet={[704, 455]} mobile={[358, 241]} alt="VBooking Turbo Suite CRM pipeline: every lead in one Kanban view from New to Paid." />
        <figcaption>CRM pipeline — every lead in one Kanban view: New → Sent → Follow ups → Viewed → Paid.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <div class="study-pair study-pair--after">
        <figure class="study-figure">
          <StudyMedia project="vbooking" name="04-itinerary-builder" desktop={[588, 477]} tablet={[704, 571]} mobile={[358, 288]} alt="VBooking Turbo Suite itinerary builder: a day-by-day Tokyo quotation." />
          <figcaption>Itinerary builder — a day-by-day Tokyo quotation with Generate with AI.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="study-figure">
          <StudyMedia project="vbooking" name="06-turbo-suite-home" desktop={[588, 477]} tablet={[704, 571]} mobile={[358, 288]} alt="VBooking Turbo Suite home: Ask Genie, latest offers and sales analytics." />
          <figcaption>Turbo Suite home — Ask Genie, latest offers and sales analytics.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="study-section study-section--muted" aria-labelledby="marketing-title">
    <div class="container">
      <StudyHead id="marketing-title" eyebrow="03 — Marketing site · The front door" line1="One front door for a" line2="multi-product platform" lead="VBooking sells many products to very different businesses. The homepage had to let each visitor find their own way in within a scroll." accent={false} />
      <div class="study-split study-split--marketing">
        <figure class="study-figure">
          <StudyMedia project="vbooking" name="07-marketing-homepage" desktop={[588, 627]} tablet={[704, 751]} mobile={[358, 380]} alt="The VBooking marketing homepage: product tiles, Turbo Suite by business type, and role-based entry points." />
          <figcaption>VBooking homepage.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <ol class="study-decisions study-decisions--stack">
          <li class="fact fact--md"><span class="fact__label">01</span><h3 class="fact__value">Products as one row of tiles</h3><p class="fact__text">Search engine, AI agent, holiday builder, CRM and more — the breadth of the platform reads at a glance before any detail.</p></li>
          <li class="fact fact--md"><span class="fact__label">02</span><h3 class="fact__value">Turbo Suite by who is buying</h3><p class="fact__text">Wholesale tour operators, incoming agencies, online travel agencies, holiday rental managers — each with its own pitch and a “Book Demo”.</p></li>
          <li class="fact fact--md"><span class="fact__label">03</span><h3 class="fact__value">Role-based entry points</h3><p class="fact__text">Traveler, Travel Expert, Travel Agent — the site hands each visitor straight into the right side of the platform.</p></li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <StudyQuote
    quote="Based on my long-term experience working with him, I can truly say that his creative touches are always flawless and well-crafted. He is highly professional, exceptionally cooperative, and has never fallen short at any stage. I highly recommend working with him."
    name="Omrie Abu Madi"
    role="Founder, VBooking · Dubai, UAE"
    initials="OA"
  />

  <section class="study-section" aria-labelledby="outcome-title">
    <div class="container">
      <StudyHead id="outcome-title" eyebrow="04 — Outcome" line1="Both sides live in beta" />
      <div class="study-outcome">
        <div>
          <h3>Outcome</h3>
          <p class="study-outcome__lead">Both sides shipped as live betas, active but not yet open to the general market. Throughout the project I reviewed each stage with VBooking’s stakeholders — their feedback shaped the three-entry-point approach to planning and showed where the old tool-hopping workflow still leaked into the new one, and it now guides the run-up to a full launch.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>What I learned</h3>
          <p>Designing both platforms at once made it clear that a traveler, a travel expert and a travel agent are three different people even when they’re looking at the same trip. Heavy competitor analysis shaped where VBooking chose to differentiate: pairing AI planning with a real human behind every quote, and a unified CRM pipeline instead of copying what every other booking tool already did.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <StudyCta title="Building a platform with more than one side?" text="Tell me who it serves, and where their journeys meet." />
</article>

<StudyNext current="vbooking" />

</Layout>
